// D0161 — PROPOSED, SECOND DRAFT. The first draft designed a store for ingesting EXTERNAL documents and
// the human called it low value; they want keel to explain ITSELF — why it is designed this way, what the
// needs were, so that reasoning drives decisions. That reasoning is ALREADY authored. So this draft is not
// a knowledge base, it is a RETRIEVAL INDEX over the model that exists, and both invariant exceptions the
// first draft needed disappear.
// #ProspectiveChange (D0070): adds a small directory and two item types; derives no requirement yet.
package Decision0161 {
    private import EngineElement::*;
    private import EngineWork::*;
    private import EngineVerification::*;
    private import EngineRelationships::*;

    #ProspectiveChange part d0161 : Decision {
        :>> id = "b8407e15-3c69-4d2a-9016-5ea7f3c04b9d";
        :>> title = "D0161: `.knowledge/` is a retrieval INDEX over the existing model — declared questions plus a lexicon, not a second corpus";
        :>> createdAt = "2026-08-18";
        :>> createdBy = "claudeOpus5";
        :>> status = DecisionStatus::proposed;
        :>> context = "MY FIRST DRAFT ANSWERED THE WRONG QUESTION AND THE HUMAN SAID SO ('hmm. low value?'). It designed `.knowledge/` as a store for ingesting EXTERNAL documents — a third model with its own schema, computed identity and a JSONL fact corpus — because that is what D0153's process describes. What they actually want is narrower and more valuable: 'I would like keel to store knowledge about itself, why it is designed the way it is, needs, etc. because that is the info I want to know and have drive decisions.' THAT INFORMATION IS ALREADY AUTHORED, and measured: 161 Decisions each carrying BOTH a context and a rationale field, 67 Need statements, 88 SystemRequirement rationales, 142 Issue descriptions, 341 sprint records whose gates record why each phase was judged as it was, and 1540 typed edges connecting them. The traversal half exists too — element_neighbourhood, need_slice, item_detail, grammar_json and display_label are all built and shipping. SO NOTHING NEEDS INGESTING. What is missing is the ability to ASK: there is no way to put a question to the corpus and get the reasoning back, so today the answer is a grep across 161 decision files, which is why the reasoning does not drive decisions the way it should. This is the second time this session I produced a coherent design for a question nobody asked — the first was four options for issue132, all framed around the wrong object — and the shape of the error is the same: I designed from the process I had read instead of from the outcome they wanted.";
        :>> decision = "PROPOSED: `.knowledge/` holds a RETRIEVAL INDEX over the existing model, not a second corpus. TWO SMALL HUMAN-OWNED PARTS, both SysML so the existing vocabulary and marker guards can see them: (1) `.knowledge/questions/*.sysml` — declared Question items, the questions keel must be able to answer about itself ('why is identity a UUID?', 'what forbids a second write path?', 'which Decision governs the console?'), because D0153 step 1 makes questions drive everything and because a declared question is what makes coverage measurable rather than a feeling; (2) `.knowledge/lexicon/*.sysml` — declared Alias items mapping the words a human actually uses to the elements that answer them ('identity' -> the schema clause and the Decisions that set it; 'rebase' -> D0129 and issue071), which is the one genuinely missing authored artifact and is D0153's third fact shape. THE GRAPH ITSELF IS NOT STORED. Entities are the items already in .tracking and .engine; relations are the 1540 typed edges already authored; the answer text is the context and rationale fields already written. NEW COMPUTED VIEWS, never authored: `keel why <term-or-question>` — seed by matching the term against element names, displayLabels and declared aliases, traverse the typed edges from the seed, and return the reached elements' reasoning WITH provenance (which Decision, who accepted it, at which commit) AND with any FAILING critique recorded against them, so an answer that rests on a rationale a critic has already faulted says so; and `keel knowledge question-coverage` — for each declared Question, whether seeding finds an entity and traversal reaches an answer, which can report that the corpus CANNOT answer a question the human wrote down. ACCEPTANCE IS D0153's TRAP TEST: a declared question whose answer requires chaining elements that share no words, which keyword search cannot reach and traversal can.";
        :>> rationale = "WHY THIS IS THE RIGHT SHAPE AND THE FIRST DRAFT WAS NOT: the value the human named is answerability, not accumulation. A store to fill would have started empty and stayed empty here, because keel's domain knowledge IS its decisions — so the first draft would have built a second corpus beside a full one. BOTH INVARIANT EXCEPTIONS DISAPPEAR, which is the strongest evidence the shape is right rather than merely smaller. There is no third MODEL: `.knowledge/` holds an index that REFERENCES the existing model, so the two-model rule holds untouched and /api/scope needs no third classification. There is no COMPUTED IDENTITY: the entities are existing items that already carry minted UUIDs, and the questions and aliases are hand-authored items that take UUIDs like everything else, so §1.3 holds untouched. A design that needs two exceptions to stated invariants and a design that needs none, for the same stated purpose, is not a close call. WHY SYSML AND NOT JSONL, reversing the first draft: the volume argument was the whole case for JSONL, and it evaporates — a question set and a lexicon are tens of items authored by a human, not thousands extracted by a machine, so they belong in the same form as every other governance artifact where the marker, attribute-vocabulary and edge-endpoint guards already apply. WHY THE ANSWER MUST CARRY ITS CRITIQUES: this session recorded 201 critique results, 127 of them failing, several against the very rationale fields `keel why` would return — including a Need whose justifying clause was stale and a Decision whose title contradicted its own status. An index that returned that prose as settled truth would launder findings the model already holds; returning the failing critique alongside it makes the answer as honest as the corpus. THE HONEST LIMIT, stated because it is the thing most likely to disappoint: this makes the corpus ANSWERABLE, not CORRECT. `keel why` faithfully returns what was written, including what was written badly, and the critique corpus is the only thing standing between a confident retrieval and a confident error.";
        :>> consequences = "IF ACCEPTED: a small directory, two item types with a tiny schema, and two computed views. No third model, no schema/core change, no exception to any invariant, and /api/scope untouched. The lexicon becomes a maintained artifact — an alias pointing at a superseded element must be reported rather than silently answered, which the existing supersede edges make computable. THE FIRST REAL TEST is available immediately and is the reason to build it: this session produced Decisions whose reasoning a future reader will need — why identity is minted, why history is never rewritten, why the console is local-only, why warning-level controls never got promoted — and every one of those is a question that can be declared and measured today. IF REJECTED: the reasoning stays in 161 files, retrievable by grep, and the human's stated want goes unserved — which should be recorded against this Decision rather than left as a gap, because the corpus keeps growing and the cost of not being able to ask it grows with it. EXPLICITLY OUT OF SCOPE: ingesting external documents (that is D0153's process and it can reuse this index later), recall injection into the harness prompt path, and any tier-2 or tier-3 store.";
    }
}
